  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe current best upper bound for the Erdos constant is 0.38085, and we aim to improve upon this by developing a novel optimization strategy based on:\n1. **Coarse-to-Fine Multi-resolution Optimization**: Instead of fixing a single resolution, we optimize step functions on progressively finer grids (powers of two) to escape local minima.\n2. **Adaptive Basis Representation**: Represent the step function using a piecewise linear basis derived from its initial form, reducing effective dimensionality.\n3. **Repair Mechanisms**: Keep solutions feasible by ensuring integral constraints and bounds during optimization.\n4. **Structured Local Search**: Use gradient-based optimization with temperature schedules for smooth transitions between states.\n\nOur approach differs from the parent by:\n- Using a scalable grid-based representation rather than 960-point fixed resolution\n- Employing convex relaxation and barrier methods for constraints\n- Incorporating temperature annealing schedules for better exploration\n\nMathematical Specification\nDefine the function space as:\n- Variables: Coefficients of piecewise linear basis functions basis(x) = [1, x, x\u00b2, ..., x^{m}]\n- Objective: C5 = max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed via discrete integration\n- Constraints: \u222b h(x) dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n- Optimization: Minimize the temperature-enhanced C5 measure using projected gradient steps\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Coarse-Start with initial_h_values resampled to 64-point grid\n2. Iterative-refinement:\n   - Define basis matrices for grids of size 64, 128, 256,...\n   - Project high-res constraints to low-res spaces\n   - Apply alternating optimization between basis coefficients\n   - Use repair() to maintain feasibility\n3. Repair functions:\n   ```python\n   def repair(h, n):\n       target = n/2.0\n       s = np.sum(h)\n       delta = target - s\n       if abs(delta) > 1e-6:\n           h += delta/(n)  # Even distribution\n           h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n       return h\n   ```\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- Base grid sizes: 64, 128, 256\n- Initial temp: 100\n- Temp decay: 10% per 10 steps\n- Gradient adjustments based on improvement rate\n\nComputational Budget\n- Time focus: 1000 steps \u00d7 12 grids = 12k evaluations\n- Repair operations: prioritize grid sizes under wall-clock limit\n- Fallback: Return best seen if time expires\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n- Candidates must satisfy: sum(h) = n/2+\u03b5, 0\u2264h\u22641+\u03b5\n- Objective recomputed with base compute_c5()\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
